WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:07.474
In an effort to fight climate change,

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:11.578
the Sahara Desert could 
be going green... literally.

00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:15.348
Plans are being made to terraform
the entire Sahara desert;

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:20.220
changing it from a dry, barren 
landscape to a lush green space.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.889
If successful, the
transformation could remove

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:26.726
7.6 billion tons of atmospheric carbon yearly.

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:32.298
How could we change the nature
of such a vast, isolated landscape?

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:43.143
This is WHAT IF,

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:47.480
and here's what would happen
if we terraformed the Sahara Desert.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:52.519
The Sahara desert is 8.6 million km² 
(3.32 million mi²) in size.

00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:54.521
It’s roughly the size of America —

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:57.724
if you filled America with sand 
and took away all of the trees. 

00:00:58.525 --> 00:01:01.995
Terraforming an area this
massive wouldn't be easy,

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:05.465
in fact, it would cost about $2 trillion a year,

00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:11.638
and unfortunately, the price tag would 
be just the beginning of our obstacles.

00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:16.109
What kind of environmental
domino effect would this create?

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:18.511
Plants and trees are 
the lungs of the Earth,

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:21.481
and right now we could use a lot more of them.

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:23.750
A single hectare of trees

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.786
can absorb the same amount of
carbon dioxide you would produce

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:30.557
by driving a car for 100,000 kilometers 
(62,000 miles). 

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:33.326
If we could successfully terraform the Sahara,

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:36.062
it would result in millions of hectares of trees

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:38.965
being added to the battle
against climate change.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:43.970
That all sounds great, but what are the odds
we could pull this kind of transformation off?

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:49.275
Believe it or not, we already have,
just on a smaller scale.

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:53.613
China’s Kubuqi Ecological Restoration 
Project saw the successful greening

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:55.748
of one-third of the Kubuqi Desert

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:59.586
with 70 different plant species
over a 30-year timespan.

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:03.790
How could we scale that up for 
the largest hot desert on Earth?

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.926
One idea is to plant
crops and trees, and then

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:10.697
pump desalinated water from the coast 
of the Sahara to irrigate them.

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:12.932
To prevent evaporation,

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:17.537
the water would be carried by underground 
pipes to reach the roots directly.

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:21.975
The ideal things to plant would be
eucalyptus trees since they're hardy,

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:24.277
and they do well in hotter climates.

00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:29.282
Plus, they grow quickly and could 
be economically beneficial for the region.

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:32.018
As the trees began to root and stabilize,

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:35.221
the soil would be replenished
with needed nutrients,

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:37.056
rainfall amounts would increase,

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:42.095
and the overall temperature of the 
Sahara would cool by 8°C (14.5°F).

00:02:42.095 --> 00:02:46.466
Ok, so with time running out in
our fight against climate change,

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:50.170
why aren't we moving faster on
such a significant potential solution?

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:56.743
Well first off, did we mention that
it would cost $2 trillion a year?

00:02:56.743 --> 00:03:00.146
Have fun getting international
governments to pitch in on that,

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:03.116
especially if it's just for the 
greater good of humanity.

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:05.318
But even if we could afford it,

00:03:05.318 --> 00:03:09.622
terraforming the Sahara Desert would
come with its fair share of issues.

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:14.160
As the region becomes wetter as a 
result of millions of new trees being planted,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.197
the risk of locust plagues increases.

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:19.299
Yeah that's right, locusts:

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:22.502
the swarming pests best known 
for their biblical associations. 

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:29.275
Wait, locusts can't be that bad, can they?

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:34.280
Well, a small swarm eats more than 
what 2,500 people can eat in a day,

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.382
so yeah, they can be that bad.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:39.552
However, the biggest problem
with terraforming the Sahara

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:42.322
would be the environmental
domino effect it would create.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:46.960
The Sahara’s sand gets carried
in the air by wind power

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.297
and is deposited in South America 
after crossing the Atlantic Ocean. 

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:56.035
The dust picks up moisture during its journey, 
and when it falls from the sky –

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:58.071
rain comes along with it.

00:03:58.071 --> 00:04:02.175
This dust and rain combo
falls on the Amazon rainforest,

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:06.746
helping to fertilize it and providing 
the ecosystem the water that it needs.

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:11.150
No Sahara could potentially mean 
no more Amazon rainforest

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.587
unless someone else steps in 
with a plan to avert that crisis. 

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:19.125
So even though a green Sahara
would lower our carbon emissions,

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:23.229
would it be worth potentially
destroying another part of the planet?

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:26.633
Maybe instead of terraforming
one giant location,

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:30.737
we should spread our 
greening out around the world,

00:04:30.737 --> 00:04:33.840
but that's a topic for another WHAT IF.

